data a_grounded_natural_cast_onto_the_kernel_integer_is_unjudged: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_grounded_natural_cast_onto_the_kernel_integer_is_unjudged" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "a declared, named, checked conversion from std.nat Nat onto the kernel Int (refusing outside the 64-bit range) that every such cast migrates to, after which v1.compiler.infer grounded_cast_verdict refuses a grounded natural onto the kernel Int at type check and the kernel-Int arm of v1.compiler.emit cast_source_grounded_onto_target_carrier is deleted" } },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: an explicit `as` from a value whose type settles to the std.nat Nat declaration onto the kernel Int (std.types Int, the 64-bit integer, no declaration) is admitted without any judgment of the cast. The naturals are not inside the 64-bit integers, so no derived crossing admits it (std.literal_elaboration kernel_grounding_crossing answers only between grounded carriers), and v1.compiler.infer validate_cast hands it to the name-keyed chain, which abstains because Nat is outside std.coercion dag_cast_rules.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. On main the checker abstained in the same way and v1.compiler.emit had no route for the cast, so it emitted the unsupported-cast abort and the program panicked at runtime (#14545, extdeps.units.iso8601_calendar). RUNG NOW: still mitigatable, by a DIFFERENT MECHANISM, and this change is no lowering. The #14769 rework realizes the cast as the identity (the source's grounding row and the kernel-minted target, read by identity), so a cast that used to abort now runs, and its correctness rests on no Nat beyond the 64-bit range ever reaching it. That holds because every Nat producer is overflow-checked, verified by execution on main 06acbbcc48: the v1 interpreter refuses Nat + and * past i64::MAX with IntegerOverflow, the emitted Rust's v1_rt int_add and int_mul panic on the same (built and run), and a literal past the range refuses at parse. The permanent control is v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust ct_nat_beyond_the_64_bit_range_refuses_on_both_routes_test, over a real std.nat Nat fixture: the v1 interpreter refuses i64::MAX + 1 and i64::MAX * 2 with IntegerOverflow and computes an in-range Nat, and the same fixture compiled to Rust, built and run exits non-zero with an overflow panic on both and prints the in-range value. Turning the emitted + and * unchecked turns it red. Containment by checked producers is rung 1, not a judgment of the cast.",

    "POPULATION, BY A REPRODUCIBLE COMMAND AND NOT A TRANSCRIBED FIGURE (operator ruling of 2026-10-25: no census instrument; populations are a grep at a pinned revision). A grep cannot see a cast's source type, so this bounds the class from above: every authored ` as Int` outside a comment, which also counts kernel-Int sources, refinements, casts onto std.integer Int and fixture text inside string literals. Re-run: git grep -nE ' as Int\\b' <revision> -- 'dag/*.dag' 'src/v2/*.dag' | grep -vE ':[0-9]+:\\s*//' | wc -l. The migration that discharges the trigger classifies these sites by identity as it rewrites them; until then no exact count exists, and none is claimed.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the source settles to the GROUNDED std.nat Nat declaration and the target is the KERNEL Int. A cast onto std.integer Int is the derived total widening and is judged; a cast from a user-declared type spelled Nat is a nominal value and refuses; a cast the checker skips because its source is outside the cast domain is gunbc.recurring_failure_mode the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses."
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
